SAT_CHUNK("rw/central-ideas/2/0",[
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.660967c235","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data1-1","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a novel.</p><p>Mrs. Achebe had taught piano in the same small room for forty years. The upright piano had a cracked key and a pedal that squeaked, and every student eventually asked why she did not replace it. She always gave the same answer: a perfect instrument forgives too much. On this one, she said, you have to listen.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Mrs. Achebe's students prefer practicing on newer instruments.","Mrs. Achebe cannot afford to replace her old piano.","Mrs. Achebe believes that playing an imperfect piano teaches students to listen more carefully.","Mrs. Achebe plans to retire after forty years of teaching."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Her answer, \"a perfect instrument forgives too much ... you have to listen,\" explains why she keeps it. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.b967c77822","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data1-2","passage":"<p>Many people assume that desert plants survive by storing large amounts of water. Some do, but a surprising number take a different approach. Certain desert shrubs drop nearly all of their leaves during dry months, reducing the surface through which water can escape. When rain returns, they grow a new set of leaves within days and resume making food. For these plants, survival depends less on saving water than on knowing when to stop using it.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["All desert plants store large amounts of water in their stems.","Desert shrubs grow new leaves only once every several years.","Some desert plants survive dry periods by temporarily shedding their leaves rather than by storing water.","Desert plants lose more water through their roots than through their leaves."],"answer":"C","explanation":"The text contrasts the common assumption (storing water) with shrubs that drop leaves and regrow them after rain. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.e59695e27e","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data1-4","passage":"<p>Researchers studying a population of lizards on a small island noticed that the lizards' toe pads, which help them grip surfaces, were larger than those of the same species on the mainland. The island's vegetation is dominated by smooth-barked trees, while the mainland population lives mostly among rough rocks. The researchers suggest that larger toe pads may give the island lizards a better grip on the slick bark they must climb.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Island lizards may have developed larger toe pads because they need to grip smooth tree bark.","The island lizards belong to a different species from the mainland lizards.","Smooth-barked trees are spreading from the island to the mainland.","Lizards on the mainland are larger than lizards on the island."],"answer":"A","explanation":"The text links larger toe pads to the smooth bark the island lizards climb. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.2e56a4ce6a","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data1-9","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is adapted from a travel essay.</p><p>The market opens before the sun does. By five, vendors have unfolded their tables and arranged pyramids of oranges and bundles of herbs still wet with dew. By seven, the aisles are so crowded that shoppers move sideways. By noon it is over: the tables fold, the tarps come down, and a street cleaner sweeps away the last crushed petals. An hour later, no one walking by would guess that a thousand people had passed through that morning.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["The market stays open from early morning until late at night.","A busy morning market appears and disappears so completely that little evidence of it remains by early afternoon.","Vendors at the market sell mainly oranges and herbs.","The market is too crowded for shoppers to enjoy."],"answer":"B","explanation":"The essay follows the market from setup to cleanup and ends with how completely it vanishes. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.94626dda14","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data1-11","passage":"<p>When a small publisher decided to print its novels on paper made from recycled cotton clothing, some booksellers worried that readers would dislike the paper's slightly rough texture. The publisher included a short note at the back of each book explaining where the paper came from. Sales of the new editions matched those of the old ones, and many readers wrote to say that the note had made them notice and appreciate the feel of the pages.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Paper made from cotton clothing is less expensive than ordinary paper.","A publisher's switch to recycled-clothing paper did not hurt sales, and many readers came to appreciate the paper.","Booksellers convinced the publisher to stop using recycled paper.","Readers strongly disliked the rough texture of books printed on recycled paper."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Sales held steady, and readers wrote that they appreciated the pages. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.3875868a08","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data1-13","passage":"<p>In the early years of the sport, marathon courses varied widely in length, from about 40 to 42 kilometers. Records set on one course could not be fairly compared with those set on another. In 1921, athletics officials adopted a standard distance of 42.195 kilometers, the length of a well-known course from a previous international competition. Only after that decision did marathon records become meaningful across different races.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Marathon records were more impressive before the distance was standardized.","The first marathons were longer than modern marathons.","Athletics officials chose the marathon distance at random.","Standardizing the marathon's distance made it possible to compare records set in different races."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Varied lengths made comparisons unfair until a standard distance was adopted. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.8bae98daca","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data1-14","passage":"<p>A team of volunteers in a coastal town spent a summer recording every species of bird they saw along a single kilometer of shoreline. They expected to count perhaps thirty species. They counted seventy-two. Several were migrants resting for only a day or two, which the volunteers would have missed if they had visited less often. The team concluded that frequent, repeated observation reveals far more about a place than occasional surveys do.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Occasional surveys are more accurate than frequent observation.","The shoreline is home to fewer bird species than most volunteers believed.","Migrant birds never stop along coastal shorelines.","Frequent observation revealed many more bird species along a shoreline than volunteers had expected, including brief visitors."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Seventy-two species, including one- or two-day migrants, led the team to value frequent observation. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.c071c9ea4e","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data1-16","passage":"<p>Some city parks departments have begun replacing lawns with patches of native wildflowers and grasses. Lawns must be mowed, watered, and fertilized regularly, which is costly. Native plants, once established, need little water and almost no mowing. They also provide food for bees and butterflies. Critics argue that the plantings look untidy, but supporters point out that many residents have come to enjoy the changing colors through the seasons.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Native plants require more water than traditional lawns.","Some parks are replacing lawns with native plants, which cost less to maintain and support pollinators, though not everyone likes their appearance.","City parks departments plan to remove all grass from public parks.","Residents have unanimously opposed replacing lawns with wildflowers."],"answer":"B","explanation":"The text gives the reasons for the change and notes both criticism and support. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.2919ba0ab2","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data1-18","passage":"<p>When engineers designed a new high-speed train, they faced an unexpected problem: each time the train exited a tunnel at full speed, it produced a loud boom that disturbed nearby residents. The cause was air compressed in front of the train's blunt nose. An engineer who was also a birdwatcher noticed that kingfishers dive from air into water, a much denser medium, with barely a splash. The team redesigned the train's nose to resemble a kingfisher's long, narrow beak, and the booms largely stopped.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["The train's original nose was designed to resemble a bird's beak.","High-speed trains cannot travel through tunnels.","Engineers reduced noise from a high-speed train by redesigning its nose to resemble a kingfisher's beak.","Kingfishers are affected by noise from high-speed trains."],"answer":"C","explanation":"The problem (booms), cause (compressed air), inspiration (kingfisher), and solution (new nose). The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.4d1caeb276","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data2-31","passage":"<p>A composer who writes music for video games explained that her work differs from writing for film in one important way: in a film, every scene lasts exactly as long as the director decides, but in a game, the player controls how long each moment lasts. A player might spend thirty seconds or thirty minutes exploring a single room. As a result, she writes pieces that can loop smoothly or shift gradually to match what the player is doing.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Film directors often ask game composers to write their music.","Because players control the pace of a game, a game composer writes music that can loop or adapt to the player's actions.","Players prefer games with no background music.","Video game music is more difficult to perform than film music."],"answer":"B","explanation":"The difference in who controls timing leads her to write looping or adaptive music. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.cade93aaf1","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data2-33","passage":"<p>In the 1800s, many cities drew drinking water directly from rivers that also carried sewage. Outbreaks of cholera were common. When one city began filtering its water through layers of sand and gravel, cholera cases there dropped sharply, while a neighboring city that continued using unfiltered river water saw no such decline. The comparison helped persuade officials elsewhere to adopt filtration.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["A comparison between two cities showed that water filtration reduced cholera, encouraging other cities to filter their water.","Cholera outbreaks were rare in the 1800s.","Sand and gravel make water taste better.","All cities stopped using river water in the 1800s."],"answer":"A","explanation":"The city that filtered saw cholera drop; the neighbor did not; others followed. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.a312ecfe2b","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data2-35","passage":"<p>Many cities have begun replacing streetlights with LED bulbs, which use far less electricity than older lamps. Some astronomers and ecologists have raised a concern: early LED streetlights emitted a large amount of blue light, which scatters widely in the atmosphere and can disrupt the behavior of nocturnal animals. Newer LED designs produce warmer light with less blue, offering energy savings with fewer side effects.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Nocturnal animals prefer blue light to warm light.","LED streetlights save energy, and newer designs reduce the drawbacks of the blue light emitted by early models.","LED streetlights use more electricity than older lamps.","Astronomers want cities to remove all streetlights."],"answer":"B","explanation":"The text presents the benefit, the concern about blue light, and newer designs that address it. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.55e41144eb","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data2-36","passage":"<p>When a regional airline began weighing passengers' carry-on bags at the gate, travelers complained about the delays. The airline explained that its small planes have strict weight limits, and knowing the total weight of luggage helps pilots calculate how much fuel to carry. Within a few months, passengers adjusted by packing lighter, and boarding times returned to normal.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Large planes have stricter weight limits than small planes.","The airline stopped weighing bags after passengers complained.","An airline's policy of weighing carry-on bags was unpopular at first but served a safety purpose, and passengers soon adapted.","Boarding times became permanently longer because of the policy."],"answer":"C","explanation":"The complaint, the explanation, and passengers' adjustment. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.6e3c547279","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data3-48","passage":"<p>A psychologist asked participants to predict how happy they would feel a year after a major event, such as moving to a new city. Most predicted that their happiness would change a great deal, for better or worse. When the psychologist checked in a year later, most participants' happiness had returned close to their original levels. The study adds to evidence that people tend to overestimate how long the emotional effects of events will last.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["People accurately predict how they will feel a year after major events.","Happiness levels always decline over time.","People tend to predict that events will affect their happiness for longer than they actually do.","Moving to a new city usually makes people much happier."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Predictions of big, lasting change contrasted with happiness returning to near original levels. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.558c6040b3","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data3-49","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is adapted from a nineteenth-century travel diary.</p><p>We reached the pass at noon, and the guide told us to rest. I could not. Before me the valley opened like a book whose pages I had waited all my life to read: the river glinting silver, the villages no larger than thumbprints, and beyond them, rank upon rank, mountains I had known only from maps. My legs ached, but I stood until the guide took my arm and insisted that I sit.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["The writer is so moved by the view from the mountain pass that she ignores her fatigue.","The writer is disappointed by the view after a long climb.","The guide refuses to let the travelers see the valley.","The writer is too tired to look at the view."],"answer":"A","explanation":"She \"could not\" rest and stood despite aching legs until the guide insisted. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.6411272acd","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data3-50","passage":"<p>In one study, researchers placed identical bowls of candy on desks in an office, some in clear bowls and some in opaque bowls. Over several weeks, employees ate noticeably more candy from the clear bowls. When the researchers moved the clear bowls just two meters away from the desks, candy consumption dropped sharply. The findings suggest that small changes in how visible and convenient food is can significantly affect how much people eat.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Moving bowls closer to desks reduced candy consumption.","Making candy less visible or less convenient reduced how much of it people ate.","The type of candy in the bowls affected how much people ate.","Employees preferred candy in opaque bowls to candy in clear bowls."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Clear bowls led to more eating; moving them farther away reduced it. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.875865aeb4","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data3-53","passage":"<p>Early photographs often show people with stiff, serious expressions. It is tempting to conclude that people in the nineteenth century were more solemn than people today. But early cameras required subjects to stay perfectly still for many seconds, sometimes minutes, and holding a natural smile that long is difficult. Many portrait traditions of the time also associated dignity with a calm, composed face.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["People in the nineteenth century rarely smiled in daily life.","Early cameras could capture images almost instantly.","The serious expressions in early photographs likely reflect technical limits and portrait customs rather than people's personalities.","Photographers in the nineteenth century encouraged subjects to smile."],"answer":"C","explanation":"The text offers long exposure times and portrait traditions as explanations. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.ae70dcf0f3","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data3-56","passage":"<p>Many historians once believed that a certain medieval trade route declined because of a single event: the collapse of a major bridge. More recent work draws on tax records, merchant letters, and archaeological surveys of roadside inns. Together they suggest a gradual decline that began decades before the bridge fell, as new sea routes offered cheaper and faster ways to move goods. The bridge's collapse, in this view, hastened a change already under way.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Newer evidence suggests that a trade route declined gradually because of competition from sea routes, not solely because a bridge collapsed.","The trade route declined only because a major bridge collapsed.","Historians have found no records related to the trade route.","Sea routes were more expensive than land routes in the Middle Ages."],"answer":"A","explanation":"The text replaces the single-event explanation with a gradual decline linked to sea routes. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.c9d1191461","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data3-61","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a memoir.</p><p>My mother kept a garden that never looked like the ones in magazines. Tomato cages leaned at angles, the beans climbed a fence made from old bed frames, and the marigolds grew wherever their seeds happened to fall. Visitors sometimes offered to help her \"fix it up.\" She would smile, hand them a basket, and send them out to pick whatever was ripe.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["The narrator's mother valued her garden's usefulness and abundance more than its neat appearance.","The narrator's mother grew only flowers.","The narrator's mother wanted her garden to look like those in magazines.","Visitors refused to help in the narrator's mother's garden."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Instead of tidying, she sends visitors to pick ripe food. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.8994eace7c","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data3-62","passage":"<p>Many coastal towns once dumped the shells left over from seafood processing into landfills. In one bay, a restoration group began collecting oyster shells from restaurants and returning them to the water in mesh bags. Young oysters need hard surfaces to attach to, and the recycled shells gave them a place to settle. Within a few years, new reefs had formed, filtering the water and providing habitat for fish and crabs.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Oyster shells should always be sent to landfills.","Fish and crabs prevent oysters from forming reefs.","Recycling oyster shells back into a bay gave young oysters a place to grow, leading to new reefs that benefit the ecosystem.","Restaurants in the bay stopped serving oysters."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Returned shells provided surfaces for young oysters, creating reefs. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.c0bbe036f6","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data3-63","passage":"<p>For most of history, sailors navigated by the stars, but measuring longitude at sea remained extremely difficult. Longitude depends on knowing the time at a reference location while at sea, and ordinary clocks of the era lost accuracy on rolling ships. The problem was largely solved when clockmakers designed timekeepers precise and sturdy enough to keep accurate time through storms and temperature changes, allowing navigators to calculate their position far more reliably.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Longitude can be measured without knowing the time.","Ordinary clocks worked well on ships during storms.","Sailors stopped using the stars to navigate.","Accurate, durable clocks solved the long-standing problem of determining longitude at sea."],"answer":"D","explanation":"The need for accurate time and the invention of sturdy clocks. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.2842086ea1","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data3-66","passage":"<p>Wind turbines are often placed on ridges and hilltops, where winds are strongest. Some wildlife biologists raised concerns that turbines in such locations could harm migrating birds of prey, which ride the same updrafts along ridges. A study tracking hawks and eagles near one wind farm found that collisions were concentrated at a few turbines positioned directly in the birds' main flight corridors. Shutting down those turbines during peak migration weeks reduced collisions sharply while cutting the farm's yearly energy production by less than one percent.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Temporarily shutting down a few turbines in birds' main flight paths greatly reduced collisions with little loss of energy production.","All turbines at the wind farm caused equal numbers of collisions.","Wind farms should not be built on ridges or hilltops.","Birds of prey avoid wind farms entirely."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Collisions were concentrated at a few turbines, and targeted shutdowns helped. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.2d43aeb6e1","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data3-67","passage":"<p>Some languages have many more words for colors than others. For years, researchers debated whether speakers of such languages actually perceive colors differently. In one experiment, speakers of a language with separate words for light blue and dark blue were slightly faster at telling those two shades apart than speakers of a language with only one word for blue. The difference was small, but it suggests that the words people use may subtly influence how quickly they notice distinctions.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["An experiment suggests that having separate words for two shades may make speakers slightly faster at distinguishing them.","Speakers of all languages perceive colors in exactly the same way.","Some languages have no words for colors at all.","People who speak two languages cannot tell shades of blue apart."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Separate words for light and dark blue were linked to slightly faster discrimination. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.f940bc1c17","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data4-68","passage":"<p>Sea levels were far lower during the last ice age, when enormous amounts of water were locked in glaciers. Areas that are now shallow seas were once dry land. Archaeologists have begun searching these submerged landscapes with sonar and divers, and they have found stone tools and the remains of hearths beneath the seafloor. Such discoveries suggest that some early human settlements now lie underwater, which may explain gaps in the archaeological record on land.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Archaeologists have stopped searching for settlements on land.","Evidence of early human activity on land now covered by the sea may help fill gaps in the archaeological record.","Sea levels were higher during the last ice age than they are now.","Early humans lived only in areas that are still dry land today."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Tools and hearths under the seafloor suggest some settlements are now submerged. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.3eeada146d","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data4-70","passage":"<p>Researchers wondered whether dogs understand where a person is pointing or simply follow the movement of a hand. In one test, a researcher pointed at one of two cups while standing still, then lowered her arm before the dog was released. Most dogs still went to the indicated cup, even though the arm was no longer moving or extended. The results suggest that dogs interpret pointing as a signal about a location, not merely as a motion to follow.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Dogs can find hidden food only by smell.","Dogs follow any moving object regardless of where it points.","An experiment suggests that dogs understand pointing as indicating a location rather than just following hand movements.","Dogs ignore people who point at objects."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Dogs chose the indicated cup even after the arm was lowered. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.9cd4720ca7","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data4-71","passage":"<p>When the price of a city's public transit passes was cut in half for residents over sixty-five, officials expected ridership among older residents to rise modestly. It nearly doubled. Surveys revealed that many older residents had wanted to use transit more but had been deterred by cost. Several also reported visiting friends, libraries, and parks more often, suggesting that the policy affected not only transportation but also social life.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Most older residents already used transit as much as they wanted.","Cutting transit fares for older residents greatly increased their ridership and appeared to broaden their social activities.","Older residents stopped using public transit after the price change.","The city doubled transit fares for residents over sixty-five."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Ridership nearly doubled, and residents reported visiting people and places more often. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.cf6aa024e8","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data4-75","passage":"<p>A company that makes running shoes gave one group of runners shoes with thick cushioning and another group shoes with minimal cushioning. After six months, the two groups reported similar rates of injury. However, runners in each group tended to change how their feet struck the ground: those in minimal shoes landed more lightly on the middle of the foot, while those in cushioned shoes more often landed on the heel. The researchers suggested that runners may adapt their stride to their shoes.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Runners in shoes with different cushioning had similar injury rates but appeared to adjust their stride to their footwear.","Runners in minimal shoes were injured far more often.","The type of shoe had no effect on how runners' feet struck the ground.","Cushioned shoes greatly reduced injuries compared with minimal shoes."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Injury rates matched, but foot strike patterns differed. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.47049af24c","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data4-77","passage":"<p>Scientists once thought that the deep ocean floor was nearly lifeless, too dark and cold to support much activity. Then researchers exploring volcanic vents discovered thriving communities of giant tube worms, clams, and shrimp. These animals depend not on sunlight but on bacteria that draw energy from chemicals released by the vents. The discovery showed that life can flourish in conditions once considered impossible.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["The discovery of life around deep-sea vents showed that ecosystems can thrive without sunlight.","Tube worms need sunlight to survive.","The deep ocean floor contains no living things.","Volcanic vents are found only in shallow water."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Vent communities rely on chemical energy rather than sunlight. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.04beed1065","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data4-79","passage":"<p>Some bird species are known to use the sun and stars to navigate, but a study of young songbirds raised indoors suggests that they may also inherit a sense of direction. Birds that had never migrated and had never seen the sky still became restless and tried to fly in the correct migratory direction at the right time of year. The researchers propose that young birds may be born with a rough compass that experience later refines.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Songbirds must see the stars before they can navigate.","Adult birds teach young birds which direction to fly.","Young songbirds raised without seeing the sky still oriented in the correct migratory direction, suggesting an inborn sense of direction.","Birds raised indoors never try to migrate."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Birds with no experience still faced the right direction. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.0e25ffaad0","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data4-80","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a memoir.</p><p>My first job was washing dishes in a busy diner. I thought of it only as a way to earn money for a bicycle. But the cook, a woman named Dolores, noticed me watching her flip eggs and began teaching me during slow hours: how to crack an egg with one hand, how to tell by sound when the oil was hot. Thirty years and two restaurants later, I still crack eggs the way she taught me.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["The narrator used the job to save for a car.","Dolores refused to teach the narrator.","A dishwashing job the narrator took for money led to lessons that shaped a lifelong career in cooking.","The narrator never learned to cook."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Dolores's lessons led to a career in restaurants. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.0ccbedea74","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data4-82","passage":"<p>A researcher analyzing thousands of restaurant reviews noticed that reviewers used different words depending on price. In reviews of expensive restaurants, words associated with long, complex descriptions appeared more often, while reviews of inexpensive places more often compared food to comforting or addictive things. The researcher suggests that people may describe food partly to signal something about themselves, not only to describe the meal.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Restaurant reviews are mostly written by chefs.","Reviewers use exactly the same words for all restaurants.","Expensive restaurants always serve better food than inexpensive ones.","An analysis of restaurant reviews suggests that the language people use to describe food may reflect how they want to present themselves."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Word choices varied with price, suggesting self-presentation. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.0731a9acf4","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data4-83","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a poem.</p><p>My grandfather's hands were maps of work, / the knuckles hills, the scars like rivers crossed. / He never told me where he'd been, / but I could read it there: / the rope burns of the fishing years, / the calluses from building walls, / the pale half-moon a saw once carved / the winter the barn was raised.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["The speaker describes a map of the town where the grandfather lived.","The speaker learns about the grandfather's life by reading the marks of work on his hands.","The speaker's grandfather often told stories about his travels.","The speaker's grandfather worked only as a fisherman."],"answer":"B","explanation":"The hands reveal his history even though he \"never told.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.9910adac0d","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data4-87","passage":"<p>For decades, a popular brand of breakfast cereal was sold in a tall box. When the company switched to a shorter, wider box containing the same amount of cereal, shoppers complained that they were getting less. Sales dipped until the company printed the weight in large numbers on the front and added a line stating \"Same amount, new shape.\" Sales then recovered.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Shoppers preferred the new box shape from the start.","Sales rose immediately after the box changed shape.","The company reduced the amount of cereal in each box.","Shoppers mistakenly believed a reshaped cereal box contained less cereal until the company clearly displayed the weight."],"answer":"D","explanation":"The shape change caused a false impression that labeling corrected. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.cdeb8aa022","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data4-91","passage":"<p>When a city library began lending musical instruments, librarians expected mostly children to borrow them. The most frequent borrowers turned out to be adults who had played instruments decades earlier and wanted to see whether they could still play. Several formed a small band that now rehearses in a library meeting room every Thursday evening.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Adults in the city had never played instruments before.","A library's instrument-lending program unexpectedly drew many adults returning to music, some of whom formed a band.","Children were the only people who borrowed the library's instruments.","The library stopped lending instruments after one year."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Adults, not children, were the main borrowers, and some formed a band. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.c91f2c18d3","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data4-92","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is adapted from a letter written by a young teacher in the early twentieth century.</p><p>The schoolhouse has one room, one stove, and twenty-three children between the ages of six and fifteen. I teach all of them at once. The older ones help the younger with their letters, and I have come to believe they learn as much by teaching as by listening to me. Yesterday a boy of twelve explained long division to a girl of eight more clearly than I could have.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["The teacher struggles to control a large class and plans to quit.","The teacher observes that older students in her one-room school learn by helping younger students.","The school has separate rooms for each age group.","The older students refuse to help the younger ones."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Older students teach younger ones, and the teacher believes they learn by teaching. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.395d6886b8","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data4-93","passage":"<p>Scientists studying elephants noticed that the animals sometimes gathered and stood still, lifting a foot or leaning forward, even when no other elephants were visible. Recordings revealed that elephants produce very low-frequency rumbles that travel through the ground over long distances. Experiments suggested that elephants can detect these vibrations through sensitive nerve endings in their feet, which may explain the unusual postures.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Elephants lift their feet only when they are injured.","Elephants may communicate over long distances with low-frequency rumbles that they detect through their feet.","Elephants cannot hear sounds made by other elephants.","Low-frequency rumbles travel only through the air."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Rumbles travel through the ground and may be sensed through the feet. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.d8fa097250","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data4-94","passage":"<p>Many museums keep only a small fraction of their collections on display, storing the rest in warehouses. One natural history museum decided to let visitors view part of its storage area through glass walls, where scientists could be seen cataloging specimens. Visitor surveys showed that many people found the working storage area more interesting than some traditional exhibits, because it showed how museum research is actually done.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Museums display nearly all of their collections at all times.","The museum closed its traditional exhibits.","Visitors complained that the storage area was boring.","Visitors to a museum found a glass-walled storage area engaging because it revealed the museum's research work."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Surveys showed interest in seeing research in action. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.723d58e419","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data4-95","passage":"<p>Farmers in one region traditionally planted beans, corn, and squash together in the same field. Corn stalks give beans a structure to climb. Bean plants add nitrogen to the soil, which benefits the corn. Squash leaves spread across the ground, shading out weeds and helping the soil retain moisture. Each crop, in other words, helps the others grow.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Corn, beans, and squash should be planted in separate fields.","Beans remove nitrogen from the soil.","Planting beans, corn, and squash together benefits all three crops because each supports the others.","Squash leaves prevent corn from growing."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Each crop provides support, nutrients, or shade. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.6fb4c89a4e","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data5-98","passage":"<p>Ecologists studying a river noticed that trout populations were healthiest in stretches shaded by trees. Shade keeps water cooler, and cooler water holds more dissolved oxygen, which trout need. Where trees along the banks had been cleared for pasture, water temperatures rose in summer and trout numbers fell. Several landowners have since planted trees along the river to restore shade.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Trout prefer warm, sunny stretches of river.","Landowners removed trees to help trout populations.","Clearing trees along rivers increases the oxygen in the water.","Trees along a river help trout by shading the water, keeping it cool and rich in oxygen."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Shade leads to cooler, more oxygenated water and healthier trout. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.148857eaf3","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data5-99","passage":"<p>In the 1800s, many doctors did not wash their hands between examining patients. One physician noticed that women giving birth in a hospital ward staffed by doctors died of fever far more often than women in a ward staffed by midwives, who did not perform autopsies. He suspected that doctors were carrying something harmful from autopsies to patients and required them to wash their hands with a chlorine solution. Death rates in the doctors' ward fell dramatically.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Handwashing had no effect on death rates.","Midwives in the 1800s performed more autopsies than doctors.","Doctors in the 1800s always washed their hands between patients.","A physician's observation led him to require handwashing, which sharply reduced deaths in a hospital ward."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Comparing wards led to handwashing and fewer deaths. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.4b75969463","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data5-100","passage":"<p>Some sea turtles nest on beaches lined with hotels and streetlights. Hatchlings typically find the ocean by crawling toward the brightest horizon, which on a natural beach is the moonlit sea. Artificial lights can lure them inland instead, where many die. Towns that require beachfront buildings to dim or shield their lights during nesting season have seen far more hatchlings reach the water.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Hotels near beaches help hatchlings reach the sea.","Hatchlings prefer to crawl toward dark areas inland.","Artificial lighting can lead sea turtle hatchlings away from the ocean, and dimming lights helps more of them reach the water.","Sea turtle hatchlings find the ocean by following sounds."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Hatchlings follow light; dimming lights helps them. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.969b0ef665","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data5-101","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a memoir.</p><p>I learned to swim in a cold lake at the age of nine, taught by my older brother, who was eleven and far more confident than he had any right to be. His method was simple: he stood waist-deep and told me to swim to him. Each time I got close, he took one step back. I was furious. By the end of the summer, I could swim across the entire cove.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["The narrator refused to learn to swim from her brother.","The narrator learned to swim in a heated pool.","The narrator's brother was a professional swimming instructor.","The narrator's brother used a simple trick to push her to swim farther, which frustrated her but made her a strong swimmer."],"answer":"D","explanation":"By stepping back, he extended each swim, and she could soon cross the cove. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.83e60b1c21","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data5-102","passage":"<p>Many people believe that bulls are enraged by the color red. In fact, cattle cannot distinguish red from green. Experiments have shown that bulls charge at a moving cape regardless of its color, suggesting that the motion, not the color, provokes them. The red cape's use in bullfighting likely has more to do with tradition and showmanship than with any effect on the animal.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Bullfighters use red capes because bulls cannot see them.","Cattle see colors exactly as humans do.","Bulls are enraged by the color red.","Bulls respond to a cape's motion, not its color, so the red cape is more a matter of tradition than effect."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Experiments show motion, not color, triggers charging. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.607aa2fe05","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data5-103","passage":"<p>A company that designs video games tested two versions of a tutorial. In one, players read written instructions before starting. In the other, players learned controls gradually through short challenges during the first level. Players in the second group were more likely to finish the first level and to keep playing afterward. The company's designers concluded that people learn a game better by doing than by reading about it.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Written instructions were more effective than in-game challenges.","Players preferred reading instructions before playing.","The company stopped including tutorials in its games.","Players who learned a game's controls through gradual challenges were more engaged than players who read instructions first."],"answer":"D","explanation":"The challenge-based tutorial led to more completion and continued play. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.e6fe4c62cc","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data5-104","passage":"<p>Scientists once assumed that plants grow toward light simply because light speeds up growth. Later experiments revealed a more precise mechanism. A plant hormone gathers on the shaded side of a stem, causing cells there to lengthen faster than cells on the sunny side. Because the shaded side grows more, the stem bends toward the light.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Plant hormones gather on the sunny side of stems.","Plants grow away from light to avoid damage.","Plants bend toward light because a hormone makes cells on the shaded side of the stem grow faster.","Light makes all cells in a stem grow at the same rate."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Faster growth on the shaded side bends the stem toward light. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.c89ce1845e","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data5-108","passage":"<p>Honeybee colonies are sometimes described as \"superorganisms\" because individual bees function somewhat like cells in a body. Worker bees specialize in tasks such as foraging, nursing larvae, and guarding the entrance, and the colony as a whole regulates its own temperature. No single bee directs these activities; instead, they emerge from countless interactions among individuals responding to simple cues.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Worker bees each perform every task in the colony.","Bee colonies cannot regulate their temperature.","The queen bee directs every task in the colony.","A honeybee colony behaves like a single organism, with coordinated activity emerging from interactions among bees rather than central control."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Specialization and self-regulation arise without a leader. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.6802999acc","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data5-109","passage":"<p>A study of college students found that those who wrote down three things they were grateful for each week reported higher life satisfaction after ten weeks than students who wrote about daily hassles or neutral events. The effect was modest, and the researchers cautioned that the study relied on students' own reports of how they felt.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Writing about daily hassles increased life satisfaction the most.","Gratitude journaling had a dramatic effect on students' health.","Writing about gratitude was linked to modestly higher reported life satisfaction, though the findings relied on self-reports.","Students who wrote nothing were the happiest."],"answer":"C","explanation":"The gratitude group reported slightly higher satisfaction, with a caution about self-report data. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.0d07b3c889","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data5-113","passage":"<p>A newspaper columnist argued that handwriting instruction wastes class time now that most writing is done on keyboards. A literacy researcher responded with evidence from studies of young children. Children who practiced forming letters by hand recognized letters more quickly and remembered spellings better than children who practiced only by typing. Handwriting, the researcher concluded, may support reading and spelling even if adults later write mostly by keyboard.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["The columnist and the researcher agreed that handwriting should be eliminated.","Adults write mainly by hand.","A researcher cited evidence that practicing handwriting may help young children learn letters and spelling, countering a claim that it wastes time.","Children who typed learned letters faster than those who wrote by hand."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Evidence about letter recognition and spelling supports handwriting practice. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.3907130a22","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data5-114","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a poem.</p><p>The oak does not hurry. / It adds a ring a year, / a thin brown line of patience / no one sees. / Storms come, and summers, / and the children who climbed it / grow tall and move away, / and still it adds its ring.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["The poem describes an oak tree destroyed by a storm.","The poem describes the oak's slow, steady growth, which continues regardless of what happens around it.","The oak grows quickly compared with other trees.","The speaker regrets climbing the oak as a child."],"answer":"B","explanation":"\"It adds its ring\" despite storms and passing years. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.8be68d26c3","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data5-115","passage":"<p>Many people think of deserts as places with no water, but some desert animals obtain nearly all the moisture they need from food. Kangaroo rats, for example, rarely drink. They get water from seeds, and their kidneys produce extremely concentrated urine, conserving what little water they take in. They also spend the hottest hours in cool burrows, reducing moisture lost through breathing.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Desert animals cannot survive without drinking water.","Kangaroo rats drink large amounts of water each day.","Kangaroo rats spend the hottest hours above ground.","Kangaroo rats survive in deserts by getting water from food and conserving it in several ways."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Seeds, kidneys, and burrows help them manage water. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.86df492870","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data5-116","passage":"<p>When a public radio station began publishing transcripts of its programs online, some staff feared that people would read instead of listen, reducing the audience. Traffic data showed the opposite. Many readers who found transcripts through internet searches clicked to listen to the original broadcasts, and the station's online listening hours increased.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Publishing program transcripts online attracted new listeners rather than reducing the radio station's audience.","Staff unanimously supported publishing transcripts.","The station stopped publishing transcripts because of low traffic.","Transcripts caused listeners to stop listening to the radio."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Search readers clicked to listen, raising listening hours. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.7e497b547d","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data5-117","passage":"<p>Paleontologists once imagined large dinosaurs as slow, cold-blooded animals that dragged their tails. Evidence from fossil trackways, which show tails held off the ground, and from bone growth rings, which suggest rapid growth, has changed that view. Many scientists now picture dinosaurs as active animals, some possibly warm-blooded, with tails held out for balance.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Scientists agree that all dinosaurs were cold-blooded.","Fossil evidence has led scientists to see many dinosaurs as active animals rather than slow, tail-dragging ones.","Fossil trackways show that dinosaurs could not walk.","Dinosaurs dragged their tails along the ground."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Trackways and growth rings changed the older view. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.d28c9ee25b","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data5-119","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is adapted from a nineteenth-century novel.</p><p>Mr. Ashworth prided himself on never changing his mind. He had decided at twenty that the new railway would ruin the countryside, and at seventy he still refused to ride it. When his granddaughter fell ill in the city, however, he was seen at the station before dawn, buying a ticket with shaking hands.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Mr. Ashworth has always enjoyed riding the railway.","Mr. Ashworth abandons a lifelong stance when his granddaughter needs him.","Mr. Ashworth's granddaughter builds a railway.","Mr. Ashworth refuses to visit his granddaughter."],"answer":"B","explanation":"He buys a ticket to reach her despite decades of refusal. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.026b2d8a81","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data5-120","passage":"<p>Beekeepers in one region reported that their honey tasted different from year to year. Chemists analyzing samples found that the flavor compounds in the honey closely matched the flowers that had bloomed most heavily each season. In years when wild thyme was abundant, for example, the honey contained compounds associated with thyme's distinctive flavor.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Chemists could not identify any flavor compounds in the honey.","Beekeepers add thyme to honey to change its flavor.","Variations in honey flavor reflect which flowers bloomed most heavily in a given season.","Honey tastes the same regardless of which flowers bees visit."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Flavor compounds matched each season's dominant flowers. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.25249ab86d","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data6-124","passage":"<p>When a hospital added a small garden where patients could sit outdoors, staff expected it to be used mainly by visitors. Instead, nurses began bringing patients recovering from surgery outside for short periods. Records later showed that these patients requested less pain medication on average than similar patients who stayed indoors, though the researchers noted that the patients who went outside may have been healthier to begin with.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Spending time outdoors doubled patients' need for pain medication.","The hospital garden was used only by visitors.","Researchers proved that gardens cure illness.","Patients who spent time in a hospital garden used less pain medication, though this may partly reflect differences in their health."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Less medication was requested, with a caution about health differences. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.3ffba14e34","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data6-127","passage":"<p>In the 1970s, a city tore down an elevated highway that divided its waterfront from downtown. Many business owners predicted traffic chaos. Traffic did shift to other streets, but congestion did not increase as much as feared, partly because some drivers changed their routes or schedules and some switched to transit. The land along the water became a popular promenade lined with restaurants.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Tearing down the highway caused severe traffic jams for decades.","The city built a larger highway along the water.","Removing an elevated highway did not cause the predicted traffic chaos and opened the waterfront to public use.","Business owners supported removing the highway."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Congestion rose less than feared, and the waterfront was revitalized. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.ce521a9008","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data6-128","passage":"<p>Some animals appear to understand basic numbers. In one experiment, researchers trained chicks that were only a few days old to find food behind a screen marked with a certain number of dots. When the number of dots stayed the same but the dots' size and spacing changed, the chicks still chose correctly most of the time. This suggests that the chicks were responding to quantity rather than to the overall appearance of the pattern.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Chicks prefer larger dots to smaller dots.","Chicks cannot distinguish between different numbers of objects.","Chicks learn numbers only after several months.","Young chicks appear able to recognize a quantity of dots even when the dots' appearance changes."],"answer":"D","explanation":"They chose correctly despite changes in size and spacing. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.1cd3da7203","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data6-129","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is adapted from an essay.</p><p>We tend to think of maps as neutral pictures of the world, but every map involves choices. A mapmaker decides what to include and what to leave out, which places deserve large labels, and what to put at the center. A subway map that shows stations evenly spaced, for instance, is useful for riders precisely because it distorts real distances.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Maps always show distances accurately.","Subway maps are less useful than street maps.","Maps are not neutral, because mapmakers make choices about what to show and how, sometimes distorting reality to make maps useful.","Mapmakers include every feature of a place."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Every map reflects choices; subway maps distort usefully. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.c010ec4817","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data6-130","passage":"<p>When a regional power company offered customers a discount for using less electricity during late afternoon peak hours, officials expected only a small response. Many households began running dishwashers and washing machines later in the evening instead. Peak demand fell enough that the company postponed building a new power plant it had planned.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["A discount for reducing peak-hour electricity use shifted household habits enough to delay a planned power plant.","Households used more electricity during peak hours.","The company built a new power plant earlier than planned.","Customers ignored the company's discount."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Shifted usage lowered peak demand and delayed construction. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.bcf3be4e65","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data6-136","passage":"<p>A company that manufactures outdoor jackets began offering free repairs for life. Executives worried that the program would reduce sales, since customers would buy fewer new jackets. Instead, sales rose. Surveys suggested that customers valued the promise that their jackets would last, and many chose the brand specifically because of the repair program.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Executives canceled the repair program.","The repair program caused the company's sales to fall.","Customers stopped buying jackets from the company.","A lifetime repair program increased a jacket company's sales because customers valued durability."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Sales rose as customers chose the brand for its repair promise. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.60f8165e3d","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data6-137","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a poem.</p><p>My mother's garden in December / is only sticks and string, / the tags still marking rows / of what the ground will someday bring. / She walks it anyway each morning, / hands deep in her coat, / reading the empty beds / like a letter no one wrote.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["In winter, the speaker's mother walks through her bare garden, imagining what will grow there.","The speaker's mother has abandoned her garden.","The garden is full of flowers in December.","The speaker plants the garden with her mother."],"answer":"A","explanation":"She walks the empty beds, marked for future growth. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.b0cb50fca9","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data6-142","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a memoir.</p><p>My father repaired watches in a narrow shop with a single window. As a child, I thought his work was boring: hours bent over tiny gears, not speaking. Only when I tried it myself, years later, did I understand. The silence was not emptiness. It was concentration so complete that the rest of the world simply stepped aside.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["The narrator never tried repairing watches.","The narrator still believes watch repair is boring.","The narrator later realizes that her father's silent work reflected deep concentration rather than boredom.","The narrator's father talked constantly while working."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Trying the work herself changes her understanding. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.0fa13596df","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data6-144","passage":"<p>A study of online product reviews found that reviews rated as \"helpful\" by other shoppers tended to mention both positive and negative aspects of a product. Entirely positive reviews were rated helpful less often, perhaps because shoppers suspected them of being exaggerated or written by the seller.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Shoppers found balanced reviews more helpful than entirely positive ones, possibly because they seemed more trustworthy.","Sellers write most online reviews.","Shoppers rated entirely positive reviews as most helpful.","Negative reviews are never considered helpful."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Mixed reviews were rated helpful more often. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.9f25834481","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data6-146","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a novel.</p><p>The new teacher did not take attendance by reading names. Instead, on the first morning, she asked each student to say one thing they had noticed on the way to school. The answers were slow at first: a red car, a barking dog. By Friday, students were describing the shape of clouds, the smell of rain on hot pavement, and the exact number of steps from the bus stop to the door.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Students stop noticing anything on the way to school.","The teacher reads each student's name aloud.","Students refuse to participate in the teacher's attendance routine.","A teacher's unusual attendance routine gradually makes her students more observant."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Answers grow more detailed by Friday. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.51832f626a","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data6-148","passage":"<p>A researcher studying how people choose where to sit in cafés found that most customers first selected seats along walls or near windows, leaving tables in the middle of the room empty until the café was crowded. The researcher suggests that people may prefer seats that offer a view while protecting their backs, a preference that may reflect a long-standing wariness of open spaces.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Café customers tend to choose seats along walls or windows first, possibly reflecting a preference for protected positions with a view.","Cafés should remove wall seating.","Customers prefer tables in the center of cafés.","Café customers choose seats randomly."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Wall and window seats fill first. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.b4bef7960b","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data6-149","passage":"<p>For centuries, scholars believed that a certain medieval poem was the work of a single author. A computer analysis of word choices and sentence patterns found distinct differences between the first half of the poem and the second. Some scholars now suggest that a second poet completed the work, though others argue that a single author's style may simply have changed over many years of writing.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Scholars agree that three poets wrote the poem.","The poem's two halves are identical in style.","The computer analysis proved that the poem had one author.","A computer analysis revealed stylistic differences in a medieval poem, leading to debate about whether it had one author or two."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Differences prompt two competing explanations. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.5bdc4aa257","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data7-150","passage":"<p>When a university cafeteria stopped offering trays, officials hoped to save water used for washing them. They also found that food waste dropped by about a quarter. Without trays, students carried fewer dishes at once and tended to take only what they planned to eat, returning for seconds if they were still hungry.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Removing trays from a cafeteria saved water and also reduced food waste as students took less food at a time.","The cafeteria began washing trays more often.","Students stopped eating in the cafeteria after trays were removed.","Removing trays increased food waste in the cafeteria."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Waste dropped because students took only what they planned to eat. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.7c421d08f2","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data7-152","passage":"<p>Researchers studying sleep in dolphins found that the animals rest one half of their brain at a time while the other half stays alert. During this state, a dolphin keeps one eye open, surfaces regularly to breathe, and continues to watch for threats. After a period of time, the halves switch roles.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Dolphins rest both halves of the brain at once.","Dolphins rest one half of the brain at a time, which lets them keep breathing and watching for danger.","Dolphins sleep with both eyes closed at the bottom of the ocean.","Dolphins never sleep at all."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Alternating halves allow breathing and vigilance. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.9b0b684098","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data7-158","passage":"<p>Researchers found that people shopping online tend to buy more when a website shows how many other people have recently purchased an item. When the counter was removed from one site, sales of those items fell modestly. The researchers suggest that people use others' choices as a shortcut when they are uncertain about what to buy.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Online shoppers ignore information about other buyers.","Shoppers prefer items that no one else has bought.","Showing how many others bought an item appears to increase purchases because shoppers use others' choices as guidance.","Removing purchase counters increased sales."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Sales fell when the counter was removed. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.1b1c365cb8","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data7-161","passage":"<p>Early astronomers noticed that the planet Mars sometimes appeared to move backward across the sky for several weeks before resuming its usual direction. Some explained this with complex systems of circles within circles. The motion is now understood as an effect of perspective: Earth, moving faster in its orbit, periodically overtakes Mars, making Mars appear to slip backward.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Earth moves more slowly than Mars.","Mars actually reverses direction in its orbit.","Early astronomers never noticed Mars's motion.","Mars's apparent backward motion is an illusion caused by Earth overtaking it in orbit."],"answer":"D","explanation":"The apparent reversal is a perspective effect. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.d04e12de2f","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data7-163","passage":"<p>Salmon that hatch in rivers spend years in the ocean before returning to spawn. When they die after spawning, their bodies decompose along riverbanks. Scientists have found traces of ocean nitrogen in streamside trees, delivered by salmon carcasses dragged into the forest by bears. The fish, in effect, carry nutrients from the sea into the forest.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Trees near rivers absorb nitrogen only from rainfall.","Bears prevent salmon from reaching rivers.","Salmon returning from the ocean deliver marine nutrients to forests when their carcasses decompose near rivers.","Salmon spend their entire lives in rivers."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Ocean nitrogen appears in trees via salmon carcasses. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.f7c96a8419","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data7-164","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a memoir.</p><p>My grandmother never threw anything away. Jars held buttons, rubber bands, and bits of string. As a teenager I found this embarrassing. Then, during a week-long power outage, our neighbors came to her door one by one, asking for candles, batteries, a length of wire, a spare key. She had everything. She handed it all over with a small, satisfied smile.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["The grandmother throws away old items before the outage.","The neighbors refuse to accept the grandmother's help.","The narrator organizes the grandmother's belongings.","The grandmother's habit of saving things, once embarrassing to the narrator, proves valuable during a power outage."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Saved items help neighbors during the outage. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.77dfa76252","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data7-165","passage":"<p>Engineers designing bridges in earthquake zones once focused on making structures as rigid as possible. Newer designs often include flexible bearings and dampers that allow parts of a bridge to move slightly during shaking. By absorbing energy rather than resisting it, these features can reduce damage to the main structure.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Newer earthquake-resistant bridges use flexible parts that absorb energy, reducing structural damage.","Earthquakes do not affect bridges.","Bridges in earthquake zones should be completely rigid.","Flexible bearings make bridges more likely to collapse."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Absorbing energy reduces damage. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.4a2b3de21f","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data7-169","passage":"<p>A city experimented with \"complete streets,\" redesigning several roads to include wider sidewalks, bike lanes, and crosswalks with shorter crossing distances. Some drivers predicted longer commutes. Travel times for cars increased only slightly, while the number of pedestrians and cyclists on those streets rose substantially, and serious crashes declined.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Car travel times doubled after the redesign.","Redesigning streets for all users slightly increased car travel times while increasing walking and cycling and reducing serious crashes.","Serious crashes increased after the redesign.","Fewer people walked on the redesigned streets."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Small car delays, more walking and cycling, fewer crashes. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.6a703fdc4b","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data7-172","passage":"<p>Many desert plants have waxy coatings on their leaves. The wax reduces water loss by sealing the leaf surface. Some plants in rain forests also have waxy leaves, but for a different reason: the slick surface helps rainwater run off quickly, preventing mold and algae from growing on the leaves.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Waxy leaves are found only in deserts.","Rain forest plants use wax to store water.","Waxy coatings increase water loss.","Waxy leaf coatings serve different purposes in different environments, reducing water loss in deserts and shedding water in rain forests."],"answer":"D","explanation":"The same trait serves different functions. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.d11b3b4a9c","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data7-173","passage":"<p>When a regional newspaper began publishing weekly articles written by high school students about their communities, editors expected little interest from adult readers. Instead, the student articles were among the most read on the paper's website, and several local officials cited them at public meetings as helpful views of issues they had overlooked.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Articles by high school students proved popular with adult readers and influenced local officials.","Local officials criticized the students for writing articles.","The newspaper stopped publishing student articles.","Adult readers ignored the student articles."],"answer":"A","explanation":"High readership and citations by officials. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.bdd8a2a2cb","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data7-174","passage":"<p>Scientists once thought that adult brains could not produce new neurons. Studies of songbirds, and later of mammals, found evidence that new neurons form in certain brain regions throughout life. In some animals, the formation of new neurons increased with learning and exercise, though researchers continue to debate how much this occurs in adult humans.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Scientists agree that adult humans produce many new neurons.","Evidence shows that some adult brains produce new neurons, though the extent in humans is still debated.","Adult brains can never produce new neurons.","Exercise prevents the formation of new neurons."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Evidence of neurogenesis, with human extent debated. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.6694fbfa0d","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data8-180","passage":"<p>For centuries, scholars assumed that a certain carved stone monument was a religious object. Recent analysis of the carvings revealed markings that align with the positions of the sun at the solstices and equinoxes. Some researchers now propose that the monument may also have served as a calendar, helping communities plan planting and festivals.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["The monument has been proven to be only a religious object.","New analysis suggests that a carved monument long thought to be religious may also have served as a calendar.","Communities never used calendars for planting.","The carvings show the positions of stars at midnight."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Solar alignments suggest a calendar function. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.2394c3fa43","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data8-188","passage":"<p>Biologists studying hibernating ground squirrels found that the animals periodically warm themselves up during hibernation, raising their body temperature for about a day before cooling down again. These arousals use a large share of the energy the squirrels spend over the winter. Researchers suspect the arousals may be necessary for functions such as sleep or immune activity that cannot occur at very low body temperatures.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Ground squirrels stay at the same low temperature throughout hibernation.","Squirrels stop hibernating after one arousal.","Hibernating ground squirrels periodically rewarm despite the energy cost, possibly to allow essential body functions.","Arousals save energy for squirrels."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Costly arousals may support vital functions. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.adce429e19","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data8-189","passage":"<p>A museum that offered free admission one evening a month found that the free nights attracted visitors who had never been to the museum before, including many families with young children. Membership sign-ups also rose after these evenings, suggesting that some first-time visitors returned as paying members.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["The museum stopped offering free admission.","Only longtime members attended free evenings.","Free monthly evenings brought new visitors to a museum, some of whom later became members.","Free evenings discouraged people from becoming members."],"answer":"C","explanation":"New visitors and rising membership. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.627fe46257","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data8-190","passage":"<p>Scientists examining ice cores from Greenland found layers of dust and chemicals that correspond to large volcanic eruptions. By counting annual layers of snow, much like tree rings, researchers could date the eruptions and compare them with historical records of cold summers and poor harvests. Several recorded famines coincided with volcanic layers in the ice.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Ice cores contain no annual layers.","Volcanic eruptions never affect climate.","Ice cores allowed scientists to date volcanic eruptions and link some of them to historical cold summers and famines.","Tree rings are found in Greenland's ice."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Dated layers matched historical famines. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.8ca05d587b","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data8-193","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a poem.</p><p>The river does not remember / the mountain it came from, / nor does it know the ocean / where it will someday run. / It only knows the stones it turns / and the reeds along its way, / and it sings to them the one song / it has: today, today.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["The river is afraid of reaching the ocean.","The river carries stones to the ocean.","The poem depicts a river that is concerned only with its present surroundings, not its past or future.","The river longs to return to the mountain."],"answer":"C","explanation":"It knows only the stones and reeds of \"today.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.44cb75e612","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data8-194","passage":"<p>Engineers testing self-driving cars found that the vehicles handled highway driving well but struggled in situations requiring interpretation of human behavior, such as a pedestrian waving a driver forward or a cyclist making eye contact before turning. Human drivers rely on such informal signals, which are difficult to program.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Self-driving cars performed well on highways but had difficulty interpreting informal human signals that drivers rely on.","Informal human signals are easy to program.","Self-driving cars struggled most on highways.","Pedestrians never signal to drivers."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Highways were manageable, but human cues were hard. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.917261966d","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data8-195","passage":"<p>Some islands have species of birds that cannot fly, even though their ancestors could. Biologists suggest that on islands without ground predators, flying offered few advantages while requiring a great deal of energy. Over many generations, birds that spent less energy on flight muscles and more on other traits may have survived and reproduced more successfully.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Flightless island birds may have evolved because flying offered little advantage where ground predators were absent.","Island birds lost flight because they were hunted by predators.","All birds on islands can fly.","Flying requires little energy."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Without predators, flight's costs outweighed its benefits. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.bd84278eb3","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data8-196","passage":"<p>When a library replaced its self-checkout machines with staffed desks for a trial period, checkout times increased slightly. Surveys, however, showed that patrons asked librarians many more questions, often about finding books, using databases, or getting help with job applications. Several patrons said they had not realized librarians could help with such tasks.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Self-checkout machines led to more questions.","Librarians could not help with job applications.","Staffed checkout desks slowed checkout slightly but led patrons to ask librarians for many kinds of help.","Patrons refused to use staffed desks."],"answer":"C","explanation":"More interaction revealed librarians' services. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.27b9728866","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data9-197","passage":"<p>When a coastal city's fishing industry declined, many old wooden boats were left to rot in the harbor. A group of retired shipwrights began restoring a few of them and offering free sailing lessons to local teenagers. The project grew into a program that now teaches boatbuilding skills, and several graduates have found jobs at repair yards along the coast.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["A volunteer effort to restore old boats grew into a program that teaches teenagers skills leading to jobs.","Retired shipwrights sold the old boats.","Teenagers refused to learn sailing.","The city's fishing industry recovered after boats were restored."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Restoration became training and employment. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.c137c2a24b","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data9-199","passage":"<p>Researchers who installed underwater microphones near a restored oyster reef found that the reef grew noisier over several years, filled with the clicks and grunts of fish and shrimp. The noise levels closely tracked increases in the numbers of animals divers counted. The researchers suggest that recording sound could offer a cheaper way to monitor reef recovery than frequent diving surveys.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Divers counted fewer animals over time.","Underwater microphones harmed the reef.","Oyster reefs became quieter as they recovered.","Sounds recorded on a restored reef tracked increases in animal life, suggesting that sound monitoring could cheaply measure reef recovery."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Noise matched animal counts. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.a069a4ecfc","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data9-201","passage":"<p>A psychologist studying how people judge the passage of time asked participants to estimate how long they had spent on various tasks. People consistently felt that time passed faster when they were absorbed in engaging activities and slower when they were bored or waiting, even when the actual durations were identical.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["People accurately estimate time regardless of their activity.","Time actually moves faster during engaging tasks.","People's sense of how much time has passed depends on how engaged they are, even when the actual time is the same.","Boring tasks seem to pass faster than engaging ones."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Perception differs from real duration. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.68a0bbcad7","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data9-203","passage":"<p>A city that offered residents free compost bins found that participation varied widely by neighborhood. In areas where volunteers went door to door explaining how composting works, many more households used the bins than in areas where bins were simply delivered with printed instructions.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Printed instructions were more effective than volunteers.","The city stopped offering compost bins.","Personal explanations from volunteers led to much higher use of free compost bins than printed instructions alone.","Residents in all neighborhoods used compost bins equally."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Door-to-door outreach increased participation. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.958e1d2231","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data9-206","passage":"<p>When an airline began allowing passengers to board from both the front and back doors of the plane, the average boarding time dropped by several minutes. Crowding in the aisle decreased because passengers seated near the rear no longer had to squeeze past those stowing bags in front rows.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Boarding through both front and rear doors reduced aisle crowding and shortened boarding times.","Passengers preferred boarding only from the front.","Boarding from both doors made boarding slower.","Aisles became more crowded with two doors."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Two doors reduced congestion. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.5cd3903c30","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data9-208","passage":"<p>When a school replaced traditional detention with a program in which students discussed what led to their behavior and how to make amends, repeat offenses declined. Teachers noted that students were more likely to apologize and less likely to see discipline as unfair. Some teachers, however, felt the conversations took too much time during busy school days.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Students refused to participate in the discussions.","A discussion-based alternative to detention reduced repeat offenses and improved attitudes, though some teachers found it time-consuming.","Traditional detention reduced repeat offenses more.","All teachers preferred the new program."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Benefits and a time concern. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.c6eafb66f1","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data9-209","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a short story.</p><p>Every summer, Grandpa Luis entered the county fair's pie contest, and every summer he lost to Mrs. Delgado. This year, when the judges announced that he had finally won, he did not cheer. He walked straight to Mrs. Delgado's table, cut a slice of her peach pie, and said, loudly enough for everyone to hear, \"Still the best in the county.\"</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Grandpa Luis celebrates loudly after beating Mrs. Delgado.","Mrs. Delgado wins the contest again.","Upon finally winning the contest, Grandpa Luis graciously praises his longtime rival's pie.","Grandpa Luis refuses to enter the contest."],"answer":"C","explanation":"He honors his rival instead of celebrating. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.2619b91e9d","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data9-210","passage":"<p>When a company introduced a policy allowing employees to work any hours they chose as long as they completed their tasks, managers worried that collaboration would suffer. Communication did become harder at first. Teams responded by agreeing on a few core hours when everyone would be available, and surveys later showed high satisfaction with the flexible schedule.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["A flexible schedule initially made communication harder, but teams adapted by setting shared core hours, and employees were satisfied.","Flexible schedules ended all collaboration permanently.","Employees disliked the flexible schedule.","The company returned to fixed hours immediately."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Early challenges were solved with core hours. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.57b8413e37","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data9-212","passage":"<p>Historians studying diaries from an eighteenth-century city found that residents frequently mentioned the price of bread. Entries describing price increases often appeared shortly before records of public protests. Some historians argue that the cost of basic food played a larger role in the city's unrest than political speeches, which receive more attention in traditional accounts.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Residents rarely mentioned food in their diaries.","Bread prices fell before protests.","Political speeches were the only cause of protests.","Diaries suggest that rising bread prices may have been a major cause of unrest in an eighteenth-century city."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Price increases preceded protests. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.085355928b","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data10-216","passage":"<p>Many hikers assume that a trail's difficulty depends mainly on its length. A park service that surveyed visitors found that elevation gain and uneven footing predicted how tiring people found a trail far better than distance did. Several short, steep, rocky trails were rated harder than much longer trails over gentle ground. The park revised its trail signs to list elevation gain alongside distance.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Visitors found rocky trails easier than smooth trails.","The park removed distance information from its signs.","Longer trails are always harder than shorter trails.","A survey showed that elevation gain and footing affect a trail's difficulty more than length, leading a park to revise its signs."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Steepness and terrain mattered more. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.6109d5f2b6","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data10-219","passage":"<p>When a city first installed bike lanes painted on the road, many cyclists still felt unsafe riding next to fast traffic. The city later tested lanes separated from cars by concrete barriers. On streets with protected lanes, the number of cyclists rose sharply, especially among people who described themselves as cautious riders.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Cautious riders avoided protected bike lanes.","Painted bike lanes attracted more cyclists than protected lanes.","Physically protected bike lanes attracted many more cyclists, particularly cautious riders, than painted lanes did.","The city removed all bike lanes."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Barriers made riders feel safer. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.f4bee244c2","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data10-220","passage":"<p>Some caves contain formations called stalactites, which hang from ceilings like icicles. They form over thousands of years as water dripping from the ceiling leaves behind tiny amounts of dissolved minerals. Because they grow so slowly, scientists can analyze their layers to learn about past rainfall and climate in the region.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Stalactites form in a few days.","Stalactites grow upward from cave floors.","Scientists cannot learn anything from stalactites.","Stalactites form slowly from mineral deposits, and their layers can reveal information about past climate."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Slow growth preserves climate records. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.2ae409b22e","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data10-221","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a memoir.</p><p>My grandfather taught me to fish on a lake so quiet that you could hear a dragonfly land. He never seemed to care whether we caught anything. When I asked him why we kept coming back on days when the fish would not bite, he pointed his rod at the mist rising off the water and said, \"We're not really here for the fish.\"</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["The grandfather is frustrated when they catch nothing.","The narrator dislikes going fishing.","The narrator's grandfather values the peaceful experience of fishing more than catching fish.","The lake is too noisy for fishing."],"answer":"C","explanation":"\"We're not really here for the fish.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.914b85a47a","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data10-225","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a poem.</p><p>The lamp in the window of the corner store / has burned each night for forty years. / Travelers who have never met the owner / measure their journeys by its light, / and though it saves no lives, / it tells them, turning the last corner, / that someone, somewhere on this street, / is still awake.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["A long-burning store lamp comforts travelers by signaling that someone is awake nearby.","The store owner turns off the lamp each night.","The store lamp guides ships safely to shore.","Travelers dislike the lamp's light."],"answer":"A","explanation":"The lamp offers reassurance. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.b833d16883","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data10-227","passage":"<p>A study of restaurant tipping found that servers who wrote \"thank you\" on customers' bills received slightly higher tips on average than servers who did not. The effect was small but consistent across many meals. The researchers suggest that small gestures of friendliness can influence how people respond, even in routine transactions.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Tipping is unaffected by servers' behavior.","The message had a large effect on tips.","Customers tipped less when servers wrote messages.","Writing \"thank you\" on bills was linked to slightly higher tips, suggesting small friendly gestures affect responses."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Small but consistent effect. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.78b8fe401f","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data11-231","passage":"<p>When a regional railway added quiet cars, where phone calls and loud conversations were not allowed, officials expected them to appeal mainly to business travelers. Surveys found that the cars were popular with a wide range of passengers, including students studying for exams and parents with sleeping infants. The railway later added a second quiet car to its busiest routes.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Only business travelers used the quiet cars.","Passengers complained about the quiet cars.","The railway removed quiet cars from busy routes.","Quiet cars on a railway proved popular with many kinds of passengers, leading the railway to add more."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Broad popularity led to expansion. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.900a461687","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data11-233","passage":"<p>Researchers studying why some people are \"morning people\" and others \"night owls\" found that genetic differences affect the body's internal clock. However, when volunteers spent a week camping without artificial light, their sleep schedules shifted earlier and became more similar to one another, suggesting that modern lighting widens the natural differences between people.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Camping made volunteers' sleep schedules more different.","Artificial light makes everyone wake earlier.","Genes influence whether people are early or late risers, but artificial light appears to widen these differences.","Genes have no effect on sleep schedules."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Both genetics and light play roles. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.f70691201d","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data11-235","passage":"<p>Some desert beetles collect drinking water from fog. The beetles stand on sand dunes with their backs tilted into the wind, and tiny droplets of fog collect on bumps on their shells, eventually rolling down into their mouths. Engineers have studied the beetles' shells to design surfaces that harvest water from fog in dry regions.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Desert beetles drink only from underground springs.","Fog never reaches desert dunes.","Engineers designed beetles to collect fog.","Desert beetles gather water from fog with their shells, inspiring designs for fog-harvesting surfaces."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Beetles' water collection inspires technology. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.cb79f7080f","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data11-236","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a memoir.</p><p>I spent the summer I was fourteen working at my aunt's flower shop, convinced it would be the dullest job imaginable. By August I knew the names of forty flowers, could tie a bow with my eyes closed, and had watched a nervous young man spend twenty minutes choosing roses for a proposal. I had expected to count the days. Instead, I counted the stories.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["The narrator quit the job after a week.","The narrator's aunt sold only roses.","A summer job the narrator expected to be dull turned out to be full of interesting experiences.","The narrator found working at the flower shop boring."],"answer":"C","explanation":"She \"counted the stories\" instead of the days. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.2b5130b6e5","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data11-238","passage":"<p>For centuries, farmers in some mountain regions have raised a breed of sheep with unusually thick wool that allows the animals to survive harsh winters. As many farmers switched to faster-growing breeds, the traditional sheep became rare. Recently, some weavers have begun paying premium prices for its wool, prized for its warmth and durability, giving farmers a reason to keep raising the breed.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Farmers stopped raising sheep altogether.","Weavers prefer wool from faster-growing breeds.","The traditional sheep breed has disappeared completely.","Demand from weavers for its distinctive wool is helping preserve a rare traditional sheep breed."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Premium prices encourage farmers to keep the breed. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.ccfbaeb685","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data11-240","passage":"<p>Many octopus species live only a year or two and die soon after reproducing. Female octopuses guard their eggs without eating, sometimes for months, until the eggs hatch. Researchers have found that a gland near the octopus's eyes triggers changes that lead to this fasting and decline; when the gland was removed in experiments, mothers abandoned their eggs, resumed eating, and lived longer.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["A gland in female octopuses triggers the fasting and decline that follow egg-laying.","Removing the gland caused mothers to guard eggs longer.","Female octopuses eat constantly while guarding eggs.","Octopuses live for many decades."],"answer":"A","explanation":"The gland drives brooding behavior and decline. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.34e31a263b","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data11-241","passage":"<p>When a high school changed its start time from 7:30 to 8:30 a.m., students reported sleeping about forty minutes more on school nights. Attendance in first-period classes improved, and the number of students falling asleep in class decreased. Some parents, however, noted that later dismissal times made it harder to schedule after-school activities and jobs.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Parents unanimously supported the new schedule.","Students slept less after the change.","A later school start time led to more sleep and better attendance, though it complicated some after-school schedules.","Attendance fell after the change."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Benefits plus a scheduling drawback. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.e434c82e78","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data11-244","passage":"<p>When a coastal town prohibited single-use plastic bags, many shoppers switched to reusable bags. Litter surveys found far fewer plastic bags on beaches afterward. Sales of small plastic trash bags, however, increased, as some residents had previously reused shopping bags to line household wastebaskets.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Sales of trash bags decreased after the ban.","A plastic bag ban reduced bag litter on beaches but led some residents to buy other plastic bags instead.","The ban increased plastic bag litter on beaches.","Shoppers refused to use reusable bags."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Less litter, but some substitution. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.6c9e57fa09","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data11-245","passage":"<p>Scientists studying why some leaves change color in autumn found that as days shorten, trees stop producing green chlorophyll, revealing yellow and orange pigments already present in the leaves. Red colors, however, come from pigments that some trees actively produce in autumn, possibly to protect leaves from sun damage while the trees reabsorb nutrients before the leaves fall.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Trees produce more chlorophyll in autumn.","All autumn colors are newly produced pigments.","Autumn leaf colors come partly from existing pigments revealed as chlorophyll fades and partly from red pigments trees produce.","Red pigments are present in leaves all summer."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Yellow and orange revealed; red produced. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.6f3c0efc1f","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data11-247","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a poem.</p><p>My father's workshop still smells of pine, / though he has not lifted a plane in years. / His tools hang in their outlines on the wall, / each shape drawn in pencil, / so that anything taken / would leave a ghost behind / until it came back home.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["The tools have been sold.","The speaker describes a father's workshop, where outlines of tools preserve the memory of the work done there.","The speaker dislikes the smell of pine.","The father still works in the workshop every day."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Outlines remain though he no longer works. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.5872efb07a","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data11-248","passage":"<p>Researchers analyzing thousands of hours of basketball games found that teams attempting more three-point shots scored more points per possession on average, even though three-pointers are made less often than shorter shots. Because each successful three-pointer earns an extra point, the higher reward can outweigh the lower success rate. The finding helped explain why many teams increased their three-point attempts.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Three-point shots can produce more points per possession despite lower accuracy, which helps explain their growing use.","Three-pointers are made more often than shorter shots.","Teams scored fewer points when attempting more three-pointers.","Teams stopped attempting three-point shots."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Higher reward offsets lower success. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.8760e9b4fe","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data11-249","passage":"<p>When a museum began offering sensory-friendly hours, with dimmed lights, quieter galleries, and limited crowds, officials expected to serve mainly visitors with autism. Attendance data showed that many older adults and parents with young children also chose these hours, preferring the calmer atmosphere.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Only visitors with autism attended sensory-friendly hours.","Visitors preferred crowded galleries.","Sensory-friendly museum hours attracted a wider range of visitors than expected, including older adults and families.","The museum canceled sensory-friendly hours."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Broader appeal than expected. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.d551c00438","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data11-250","passage":"<p>Geologists studying a river canyon noticed that some of the rock layers exposed in its walls contain fossils of marine animals, even though the canyon lies far inland and high above sea level. The fossils indicate that the region was once covered by a shallow sea before geological forces lifted the land.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Marine fossils in an inland canyon show that the region was once under a sea before being uplifted.","The canyon was carved by ocean waves recently.","Marine animals once lived in mountain rivers.","The fossils were carried there by people."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Fossils indicate a former sea and uplift. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.ab823fc078","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data11-252","passage":"<p>Some cities have painted crosswalks with bright, artistic designs. In one city, researchers compared intersections before and after the paintings were added and found that drivers yielded to pedestrians more often at painted crosswalks. The researchers suggest that the eye-catching designs made crosswalks more noticeable.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Painted crosswalks confused pedestrians.","Artistic crosswalk designs were associated with drivers yielding to pedestrians more often, likely because they were more noticeable.","The designs had no effect on drivers.","Drivers yielded less at painted crosswalks."],"answer":"B","explanation":"More yielding after paintings. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.5c295d82cb","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data12-255","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a novel.</p><p>Every morning, Omar's father left a folded note in his lunch bag, usually a joke so bad that Omar groaned aloud in the cafeteria. By high school, Omar pretended to be embarrassed and threw the notes away before his friends could see. Years later, packing to leave for college, he found a shoebox in his father's closet filled with copies of every note, each one dated in careful handwriting.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Omar discovers that his father saved copies of the lunch notes Omar had pretended not to value.","Omar kept every note his father wrote.","Omar's father stopped writing notes when Omar entered high school.","Omar's father threw away the notes."],"answer":"A","explanation":"The shoebox reveals the father's devotion. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.46e5ac5cb4","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data12-258","passage":"<p>Some species of fireflies synchronize their flashing, so that thousands of insects in a forest blink at nearly the same moment. Researchers suggest that synchronized flashing may help females recognize males of their own species amid the visual clutter of many flashing insects. Experiments with artificial lights showed that females responded more often to synchronized flashes than to random ones.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Synchronized flashing attracts predators to females.","Synchronized flashing may help female fireflies recognize suitable males, and experiments support this idea.","Females ignore synchronized flashes.","Fireflies flash at random intervals only."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Synchrony may aid recognition, supported by experiments. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.1c896478b9","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data12-260","passage":"<p>A study tracking two groups of patients recovering from knee surgery found that those who began gentle physical therapy within a day of surgery regained mobility faster than those who waited a week. Patients who started early also reported less stiffness. The researchers cautioned that the early therapy must be carefully supervised to avoid injury.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Waiting a week before therapy led to faster recovery.","Early therapy caused more stiffness.","Physical therapy should never be supervised.","Starting supervised physical therapy soon after knee surgery helped patients regain mobility faster with less stiffness."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Early therapy helped, with supervision. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.bec7e9c859","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data12-261","passage":"<p>When archaeologists excavated an ancient harbor, they found remains of ships loaded with pottery made in regions hundreds of kilometers away. Some pottery contained traces of olive oil and wine. The discoveries suggest that the harbor was part of a trade network that connected distant communities long before historians had assumed such networks existed in the region.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["The harbor traded only with nearby villages.","Historians already knew the network's full age.","Pottery found in the harbor was made locally.","Ships and pottery found in an ancient harbor suggest a long-distance trade network older than historians assumed."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Distant pottery indicates early trade. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.fce0792866","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data12-262","passage":"<p>When a town created a community tool library where residents could borrow ladders, drills, and garden equipment, organizers expected mainly homeowners to use it. Many borrowers turned out to be renters who lacked storage space and did not want to buy tools they would use only occasionally. The library also became a place where neighbors shared repair tips.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["A community tool library served renters as well as homeowners and became a place for sharing repair knowledge.","Renters preferred buying their own tools.","Only homeowners borrowed tools from the library.","The tool library closed because of low use."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Unexpected borrowers and a social role. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.320749b648","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data12-263","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a poem.</p><p>The kite pulls at my hand / as if it knows the sky / belongs to it, and I / am only holding the string. / I could let go. / It would climb and climb, / and I would lose it, / and I think, some days, / that would be enough.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["The speaker is afraid of flying kites.","The speaker imagines releasing a kite that seems to belong to the sky, finding a kind of contentment in letting go.","The kite falls to the ground.","The speaker ties the kite to a tree."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Letting go \"would be enough.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.4c46aea434","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data12-264","passage":"<p>Many cities have begun converting parking spaces into small outdoor seating areas called parklets. In one city, businesses next to parklets reported increased foot traffic and sales, though some drivers complained about losing parking. A survey found that most customers of those businesses arrived on foot, by bike, or by transit rather than by car.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Businesses lost customers after parklets were installed.","Most customers arrived by car.","Parklets were associated with more foot traffic for nearby businesses, and most customers did not arrive by car.","All drivers supported removing parking spaces."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Benefits and survey context. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.6f4d8d10f5","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data12-267","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a short story.</p><p>On the last day of camp, Theo's counselor handed him the old compass they had used on every hike. Theo protested that it belonged to the camp. The counselor shook his head. \"It belongs to whoever pays attention to where they're going,\" he said. Theo turned the compass over in his hands, noticing for the first time a list of initials scratched into the back.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["A counselor gives Theo a compass that has been passed down to attentive campers, marking Theo as its newest keeper.","The compass belongs only to the counselor.","Theo refuses to accept the compass.","The counselor sells the compass to Theo."],"answer":"A","explanation":"The initials suggest a tradition Theo now joins. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.b2ceca764e","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data12-269","passage":"<p>Researchers studying ancient wheat found that farmers thousands of years ago gradually selected plants whose seed heads did not shatter and scatter seeds on the ground. Wild wheat drops its seeds when ripe, which helps it spread, but that trait made harvesting difficult. By saving seeds from plants that held onto their grain, early farmers changed the crop over generations.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Early farmers gradually transformed wheat by saving seeds from plants that kept their grain instead of scattering it.","Wild wheat holds its seeds tightly when ripe.","Wheat has not changed since ancient times.","Early farmers selected plants that scattered seeds."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Selection for non-shattering seed heads. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.10b9cc9cb1","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data13-273","passage":"<p>Biologists found that some trees in dry regions shed their leaves during drought and quickly regrow them after rain, while other trees keep their leaves but close tiny pores to conserve water. Trees that drop their leaves avoid water loss almost entirely but cannot produce food until they regrow leaves. Trees that keep leaves can resume food production immediately but still lose some water.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Trees in dry regions use different strategies for drought, each with its own trade-off.","Trees that keep leaves lose no water.","All trees in dry regions keep their leaves during drought.","Trees that drop leaves produce food continuously."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Two strategies with costs and benefits. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.34e1ddd483","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data13-277","passage":"<p>A company that manufactures phone cases switched from plastic packaging to paper made from recycled cardboard. Surveys showed that customers noticed the change and viewed the brand more favorably. Shipping costs also fell slightly because the new packaging was lighter.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Switching to recycled paper packaging improved customers' views of a brand and slightly reduced shipping costs.","Shipping costs rose because paper is heavier.","Customers disliked the new packaging.","The company returned to plastic packaging."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Better perception and lower costs. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.cd1ca812f2","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data13-279","passage":"<p>When a community garden added raised beds at wheelchair height, organizers hoped to include gardeners with disabilities. The beds proved popular with many older gardeners as well, who found them easier on their backs and knees. The garden later replaced several ground-level plots with raised beds because of demand.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["The garden removed its raised beds.","Wheelchair-height garden beds benefited gardeners with disabilities and many older gardeners, leading to more such beds.","Only young gardeners used the raised beds.","The raised beds were unpopular with older gardeners."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Accessibility features had broad appeal. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.ab617c6253","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data13-282","passage":"<p>When a town's historic movie theater faced closure, residents formed a nonprofit and raised money through small donations to buy the building. They showed older films, hosted local music performances, and rented the space for community events. Within five years, the theater was financially stable and had become a gathering place for the town.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Residents saved a historic theater by turning it into a community-run venue that became financially stable.","A large company bought the theater.","The theater closed permanently.","The theater showed only new films."],"answer":"A","explanation":"A nonprofit made it sustainable. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.a3605b42e0","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data13-286","passage":"<p>Farmers in one region began releasing ladybugs into greenhouses to control aphids instead of spraying insecticides. Aphid numbers fell, and the farmers saved money on chemicals. However, ladybugs sometimes flew away when food was scarce, so farmers learned to release them in smaller groups more frequently.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Farmers switched back to insecticides immediately.","Ladybugs never leave greenhouses.","Ladybugs increased aphid populations.","Ladybugs helped greenhouse farmers control aphids and save money, though farmers adjusted their release methods."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Biological control worked with adjustments. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.9aa5338ca5","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data13-287","passage":"<p>When a city offered free public Wi-Fi in parks, officials expected people to use it mainly for entertainment. Surveys found that many users were students completing homework and job seekers submitting applications, especially in neighborhoods where many households lacked home internet.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Free park Wi-Fi was widely used for homework and job applications, especially in areas where home internet was less common.","Most households in all neighborhoods had home internet.","Park Wi-Fi was used only for entertainment.","Students avoided using park Wi-Fi."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Practical uses in underserved areas. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.cd9018fead","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data13-290","passage":"<p>When a regional power grid began paying households with rooftop solar panels and home batteries to share stored electricity during peak demand, the grid needed fewer backup power plants on hot afternoons. Households earned money, and the grid avoided building new capacity. Some households, however, worried that their batteries would wear out faster.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["The program increased the need for power plants.","Paying households to share stored solar power reduced the grid's need for backup plants, though some worried about battery wear.","Households lost money in the program.","No households had concerns about the program."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Benefits plus a battery-wear concern. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.bbd007283e","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data13-292","passage":"<p>Researchers studying why some neighborhoods have more street trees than others found that wealthier areas often had greater tree cover, partly because homeowners could afford to plant and maintain trees. In response, several cities launched programs to plant trees in neighborhoods with low tree cover and to fund their care during the first few years.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Wealthier neighborhoods have fewer trees.","Tree cover often reflects neighborhood wealth, prompting cities to plant and maintain trees in areas with fewer trees.","Tree cover is equal across all neighborhoods.","Cities stopped planting trees."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Unequal cover and city responses. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.e808aeaa3b","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data13-293","passage":"<p>When a publishing company began releasing audiobooks narrated by the authors themselves, sales of those audiobooks rose compared with versions read by professional narrators. Listeners reported that hearing authors' own voices made the books feel more personal. Some reviewers noted, however, that not all authors were skilled readers.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["All authors were excellent readers.","Author-narrated audiobooks sold better and felt more personal to listeners, though some authors read less skillfully.","Professional narrators always sold more audiobooks.","Listeners disliked hearing authors' voices."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Popularity with a caveat. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.31af130e4a","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data13-294","passage":"<p>Scientists examining ancient teeth found that people in one farming village had more cavities than hunter-gatherers who lived nearby at the same time. The farmers ate diets rich in grains, which break down into sugars that feed bacteria in the mouth. The finding suggests that changes in diet during the transition to farming affected dental health.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Ancient farmers had more cavities than nearby hunter-gatherers, likely because grain-rich diets affected dental health.","Grains prevent tooth decay.","Diet has no effect on teeth.","Hunter-gatherers had more cavities than farmers."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Grain diets linked to cavities. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.494bc3383a","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data13-299","passage":"<p>When a city introduced a program allowing residents to report potholes through a phone app with photos and locations, repair crews could plan efficient routes and fix clusters of potholes in a single trip. Average repair times fell, and residents reported greater satisfaction with road maintenance.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Residents stopped reporting potholes.","Repair times increased after the app was introduced.","A pothole-reporting app helped crews plan efficient repairs, reducing repair times and increasing satisfaction.","Crews ignored reports from the app."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Better data enabled efficient routes. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.e0195858a1","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data13-302","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a short story.</p><p>For weeks, Nadia's little brother had begged to come along on her early-morning runs, and for weeks she had said no. He was too slow, she told him. One Saturday she finally gave in. He fell behind within a block, red-faced and panting, but he did not stop. At the end of the route, Nadia found herself jogging in place, waiting, and cheering louder than she ever had for herself.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Nadia's brother runs faster than she does.","Nadia refuses to wait for her brother.","After reluctantly letting her brother join her run, Nadia is moved by his determination and cheers him on.","Nadia's brother quits the run after a block."],"answer":"C","explanation":"His persistence changes her attitude. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."}
]);
